why 1 3/4 SPACING The soprano climbs D–E–F to its peak while the alto drops to C for the F major chord. The gap lasts a beat and closes as the alto climbs C–D–E.
why 1 7/8 SPACING The same beat: the alto is climbing back up C–D–E toward the soprano.
why 2 0 DOUBLING Soprano (F–E) and alto (D–E) both move by step onto E. The soprano's F has to fall, so E is where both lines lead.
why 3 1/2 LEADING_TONE The bass walks down G–F♯–E and back up through F♯ to G. Here the leading tone is part of a passing line and reaches G two eighths later.
why 5 1/8 RANGE The bass climbs A–B–C♯–D in a long scale, up into the tenor's range, to drive toward the high point of the phrase.
why 5 1/4 DOUBLING On a weak eighth soprano and tenor both touch F♯. The tenor is passing E–F♯–G, so the doubling lasts only an eighth.
why 5 1/4 RANGE The top of the bass's long climb. Bach's basses could sing this D; in exercises, keep the bass lower.
why 5 1/2 RANGE The tenor climbs to A to stay above the rising bass and close under the high soprano. Bach's tenors sang up to A.
why 6 1/8 SPACING The alto holds a high D near the soprano while the tenor skips down D–B on its way to C♯. The gap lasts one eighth.
why 6 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION Every voice falls into the half cadence on D. The tenor's C♯, the leading tone of D, drops to A to give the chord its fifth, an accepted move in an inner voice.
why 9 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (C–B) and alto (A–B) both move by step onto B, and the alto leaves it at once for G. A doubling for one eighth, caused by smooth lines.
why 9 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION Every voice falls into ii⁶ as the phrase heads down to its cadence. The bass's leap G–C keeps the motion strong; contrary motion follows on the next beat.
